From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ncrypt: export and import keys wrapped for another key

- NCryptExportKey with an encrypting key and NCryptImportKey with a
  decrypting key answered NTE_NOT_SUPPORTED (a FIXME). The key bytes are
  now wrapped for the recipient the way key transport is done everywhere:
  a one-time AES-256 key encrypts the exported blob (CBC, PKCS7 padding)
  and the recipient's RSA key encrypts that AES key (OAEP, SHA-256).
  NCryptImportKey reverses it with the decrypting RSA key; the envelope is
  our own (Microsoft's opaque transport blob is not public and callers only
  hand it back to NCryptImportKey), so a damaged, truncated, wrong-magic
  or wrong-recipient blob fails with NTE_INVALID_PARAMETER or the
  decryption error, and a short output buffer answers
  NTE_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL with the size needed.
- A wrapped export needs only NCRYPT_ALLOW_EXPORT_FLAG on the key, not
  NCRYPT_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_EXPORT_FLAG: wrapping is the point of that
  distinction. A key that is not exportable at all is still refused.
- A wrapping key that is not RSA (AES, ECDH) answers NTE_NOT_SUPPORTED;
  a handle that is no key, NTE_INVALID_HANDLE.

Gate: test/ncryptwrap-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit, test/ncryptwrap-probe.c).
Mutants SG_MUTANT_WRAP_NO_ENCRYPT, SG_MUTANT_WRAP_POLICY (ncrypt/main.c).
---
